--TEST--
A route capture is percent-decoded exactly once as it crosses into the program, so `%20` is a space and `%2520` is the text `%20` — ADR 0102 § 1
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The decode is at the crossing and nowhere else: the segment travels from the
// matcher as the peer wrote it, and `Core\Router\Match` is where it stops being
// a piece of a URL and becomes a value. `Core\Router::match` and a served
// request's `Core\Request::route()` share that crossing, so the capture a
// handler reads is decoded once however the match was taken.
class Pages
{
    #[Core\Route(path:"/pages/{slug}", method: Core\Http\Method::Get, name: "Pages::show")]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function show(string $slug): string
    {
        return $slug;
    }
}

// One pass: the space arrives as a space.
?Core\Router\Match $spaced = Core\Router::match(Core\Http\Method::Get, "/pages/hello%20world");
if ($spaced != null) {
    echo "space: [", $spaced->param("slug") as string, "]", "\n";
}

// Once, and not "until it stops changing" -- `%2520` is an escaped `%` followed
// by `20`, so one pass answers the text `%20` and a second would answer a
// space. This is the case that tells the two apart.
?Core\Router\Match $twice = Core\Router::match(Core\Http\Method::Get, "/pages/hello%2520world");
if ($twice != null) {
    echo "once: [", $twice->param("slug") as string, "]", "\n";
}

// A path segment, not a form value: `+` is a literal plus here, and only `%2B`
// spells one on the `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` row.
?Core\Router\Match $plus = Core\Router::match(Core\Http\Method::Get, "/pages/a+b");
if ($plus != null) {
    echo "plus: [", $plus->param("slug") as string, "]", "\n";
}

// The decoded octets still have to be text: a capture binds to a declared
// `tainted string` and ADR 0009 § 1 leaves `%ff` no `string` to be, so the
// crossing throws rather than answering the escape undecoded.
try {
    ?Core\Router\Match $refused = Core\Router::match(Core\Http\Method::Get, "/pages/%ff");
    echo "unreachable", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $error) {
    echo $error->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
space: [hello world]
once: [hello%20world]
plus: [a+b]
the route capture `slug` percent-decodes to a byte a `string` cannot hold — byte 0 begins a sequence that is not valid UTF-8. A capture binds as a `tainted string`, so a path segment escaping an octet outside UTF-8 has no capture to become
